ial confronts the reader but does not leave them stranded in fear; it is explicitly reframed as information to understand and then put behind them. The SECOND INSTRUCTION arrives after the chapter has demonstrated both instinctive wisdom and human susceptibility to deception, so “KEEP AN OPEN MIND” feels earned rather than merely imposed. The final illusion preserves appropriate early-arc tension by opening the possibility that perceived pleasure is false without completing the benefits-demolition reserved for later chapters.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Assigned transition verdict:** The reader moves fully from “My craving for sweet things is a personal weakness I must control” to “My love of sweetness is natural and healthy; BAD SUGAR is a man-made substance that tricks my instincts.” The chapter maintains continuity with Chapter 1 by building on the established rejection of willpower, invoking the first instruction rather than reopening its case, and commissioning the second instruction at the chapter’s climax. Its final question creates the intended handoff: the reader has not yet accepted the full claim that BAD SUGAR provides no pleasure, but is now prepared to question that belief.\n\n**Largest momentum loss:** NONE"},"sequence_number":545}

